<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion Language Models (DLMs) introduce unique quantization challenges due to their iterative denoising process. In this work, we reveal that quantization sensitivity is both task- and time-dependent, with different downstream tasks exhibiting distinct precision requirements across denoising steps. We further show that these patterns can be predicted without ground-truth labels or task-specific evaluation metrics. Building on these findings, we introduce <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">T<sup id="abstract1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant</em>, a task and time-aware mixed-precision quantization framework that uses a label-free sensitivity signal to optimally allocate activation precision across denoising steps under a fixed bit-width budget. Extensive experiments across three DLMs and six benchmarks demonstrate that <em id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">T<sup id="abstract1.1.2.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant</em> consistently improves the average accuracy of existing quantization methods, achieving gains of up to 6.7 percentage points under the same budget.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion Language Models (DLMs) have emerged as a compelling alternative to autoregressive (AR) language models, generating text through iterative denoising with bidirectional context and parallel token prediction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. However, iterative denoising requires repeated model evaluations, making efficient inference increasingly important.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Quantization is a widely adopted technique for reducing the memory footprint and computational cost of large language models. However, methods developed for AR models may not directly transfer to DLMs due to their distinct step-wise decoding dynamics. Recent studies have identified DLM-specific quantization challenges, including activation outliers, timestep-dependent distributions, and accumulated quantization errors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu and Yang, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. While these methods improve quantization robustness, they generally maintain a fixed activation precision throughout the denoising process.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.31198v1/kl_diagnostic.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/310;" width="476" height="310"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task-dependent temporal sensitivity to activation quantization.</span>
Each curve shows, per eight-step denoising window, the KL divergence
<math id="S1.F1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p_{\mathrm{fp}}\,\|\,p_{\mathrm{q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>fp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p_{\mathrm{fp}}\,\|\,p_{\mathrm{q}})</annotation></semantics></math> summed over the positions committed
in that window that the task’s scoring function reads.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.31198v1/method_overview_v3.svg" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/142;" width="476" height="142"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S1.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T<sup id="S1.F2.3.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant</span> overview. (1) A few unlabeled prompts per task are profiled with the full-precision and quantized models. (2) Divergence <math id="S1.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> influence gives the predicted gain of upscaling each denoising window. (3) An exact dynamic program selects the windows to upscale under the bit budget. (4) The result is a task-specific schedule at the same average precision as uniform <math id="S1.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\mathrm{target}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>target</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\mathrm{target}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Meanwhile, temporal mixed-precision allocation has been explored in image diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewandowski et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, and recent studies on DLMs have highlighted how step-wise dynamics, intermediate predictions, and token commitment decisions influence generation behavior <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rahimi et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shlomi et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Nevertheless, how quantization sensitivity varies jointly across downstream tasks and denoising steps in DLMs, and how these variations can guide effective mixed-precision allocation, remains largely unexplored.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To address this gap, we systematically investigate the sensitivity of DLMs to activation quantization across different tasks and denoising steps. Our analysis reveals that <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">precision importance is both task-dependent and time-dependent (Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>)</em>: different tasks exhibit distinct temporal sensitivity patterns, with some benefiting more from higher precision during early denoising steps and others exhibiting greater sensitivity at intermediate stages. Furthermore, we find that the benefit of additional precision can be predicted without ground-truth labels or downstream metric evaluations. Specifically, we develop a label-free sensitivity signal that combines quantization-induced divergence with the downstream influence of each denoising stage, enabling the identification of task-specific precision requirements using only unlabeled calibration prompts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Building on these findings, we introduce <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T<sup id="S1.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant</span> (<span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task and Time-Aware Quantization</span>), a mixed-precision framework that allocates activation precision across denoising steps according to task-specific sensitivity patterns (Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). T<sup id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant leverages the proposed sensitivity signal to determine an optimal precision schedule under a fixed average bit-width budget through budget-constrained dynamic programming. Our framework is complementary to existing post-training quantization methods and can be integrated with different quantization approaches without modifying their underlying quantizers. Extensive experiments across three DLMs and six benchmarks demonstrate consistent improvements in average accuracy under a fixed budget, outperforming the strongest evaluated quantized baselines by up to 6.7 percentage points. Our main contributions are summarized as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task and time-dependent precision sensitivity.</span> We reveal distinct temporal precision sensitivity patterns across downstream tasks in DLMs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Label-free sensitivity prediction.</span> We predict precision importance using internal model signals, without ground-truth labels or task-specific evaluation metrics.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task and time-aware mixed-precision quantization.</span> We introduce T<sup id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant, which optimally allocates activation precision under a fixed bit budget and improves existing quantization methods.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">DLM Quantization.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">DLMs such as LLaDA and Dream generate text by iterative masked denoising with bidirectional context rather than left-to-right decoding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which raises quantization challenges absent from autoregressive LLMs. DLLMQuant introduces diffusion-aware calibration and quantization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu and Yang, 2025</a>)</cite>; STaR-Quant uses state-guided transformations and temporal compensation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2026</a>)</cite>; FAIR-Calib targets fragile denoising states <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>; and Quant-dLLM explores sensitivity-based mixed precision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026a</a>)</cite>. These methods show that diffusion state and temporal error propagation matter, yet all keep a fixed activation bit-width across the denoising trajectory. <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">We instead treat activation precision as a resource allocated over denoising steps under a fixed average bit budget</em>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Task-Aware Quantization.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Task-Aware Quantization (TAQ) estimates layer importance from a small task-specific calibration set and assigns higher weight precision to task-relevant layers under a fixed bit budget <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">LeVi et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, and Task-Circuit Quantization (TaCQ) preserves task-relevant weight circuits, identified from quantization-induced weight changes and gradient-based impact estimates, at higher precision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Both show that the best allocation of a quantization budget depends on the task, but they allocate precision <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">spatially</em>, across layers or weights of autoregressive LLMs. <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">In contrast, T<sup id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant explores temporal task dependence, allocating activation precision across denoising steps according to task-specific sensitivity.</em></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Temporal Precision Scheduling.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Temporal variation is a recognized factor in <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">image diffusion</em> quantization: Q-Diffusion uses timestep-aware calibration for shifting activation distributions, and correction methods compensate for errors accumulated across denoising steps <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Most closely related, TMPDiff assigns different precisions to different denoising timesteps in <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">image diffusion models</em>, modeling the final latent deviation as an approximately additive sum of per-timestep errors and searching over schedules built from single-timestep sensitivities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewandowski et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLMs differ qualitatively: generation consists of discrete, often irreversible token commitments, latent reconstruction error does not directly reflect downstream correctness</em>, and a timestep’s importance depends on which task-relevant tokens it commits. Rather than a single global schedule, <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">T<sup id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant allocates activation precision per task</em>, linking where quantization disrupts task-relevant generation to where additional precision should be spent.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Task-Dependent Precision Sensitivity Across Diffusion Steps</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The iterative generation process of DLMs raises a fundamental question for quantization: <em id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">when does numerical precision matter most?</em> Each denoising step operates on a different partially generated state, with token commitments potentially influencing subsequent predictions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Consequently, the impact of quantization may depend not only on the denoising step but also on the downstream task. Understanding these variations, and whether they can be predicted, motivates three research questions:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1:</span> Where does quantization sensitivity occur in time?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2:</span> Is temporal sensitivity task-dependent?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3:</span> Can we predict where additional precision is most valuable?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We address RQ1 and RQ2 through temporal divergence analysis (Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Temporal Sensitivity Diagnostic ‣ 3 Task-Dependent Precision Sensitivity Across Diffusion Steps ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>), and RQ3 through a label-free precision-allocation proxy (Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 A Label-Free Signal for Precision Allocation ‣ 3 Task-Dependent Precision Sensitivity Across Diffusion Steps ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Experimental setup.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate LLaDA-8B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>)</cite> on three tasks with distinct output structures: GSM8K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, HumanEval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, and MMLU-Pro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. We use 512-token generations over 128 denoising steps with a single 512-token block, partitioning the trajectory into 16 non-overlapping windows of eight steps. We use the standard low-confidence remasking sampler of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al. (2025)</a></cite> with temperature 0 and no classifier-free guidance. The quantized model uses QuaRot-style rotations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ashkboos et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, 4-bit GPTQ weights <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Frantar et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and dynamic per-token 4-bit activations. The target precision is <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{target}}=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>target</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{target}}=4</annotation></semantics></math> bits and the upscaled precision is <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{up}}=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>up</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{up}}=8</annotation></semantics></math> bits. Further details are given in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Experimental Setup and Implementation Details ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Temporal Sensitivity Diagnostic</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Temporal sensitivity.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows that quantization sensitivity is highly non-uniform across
the denoising trajectory. A uniform distribution would assign each of the 16 eight-step windows 6.25%
of the task-relevant divergence. Instead, on GSM8K the first window alone carries 27% (4.4<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>
uniform) and the first quarter 55%, while on HumanEval five mid-trajectory windows (steps 32–71) carry
52%. Even MMLU-Pro, the most evenly spread task, varies by nearly 2<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> between its most and least
sensitive windows. This concentration suggests that activation precision need not be allocated uniformly over time and motivates selectively increasing precision at the most sensitive stages, as explored for image diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewandowski et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="58.98" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 653.15 58.98" width="653.15"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,58.98) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#0000FF;" fill="#0000FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 8.98 L 0 50 C 0 54.96 4.02 58.98 8.98 58.98 L 644.17 58.98 C 649.13 58.98 653.15 54.96 653.15 50 L 653.15 8.98 C 653.15 4.02 649.13 0 644.17 0 L 8.98 0 C 4.02 0 0 4.02 0 8.98 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2FF;" fill="#F2F2FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.11 8.98 L 1.11 50 C 1.11 54.35 4.63 57.87 8.98 57.87 L 644.17 57.87 C 648.52 57.87 652.04 54.35 652.04 50 L 652.04 8.98 C 652.04 4.63 648.52 1.11 644.17 1.11 L 8.98 1.11 C 4.63 1.11 1.11 4.63 1.11 8.98 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 14.89 14.82)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:45.05em;--ltx-fo-height:2.54em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="37.87" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 35.18)" width="623.37"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:45.05em;">
<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00008C;">Key Takeaway<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> Quantization sensitivity varies across denoising steps.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Task-dependent sensitivity.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The location and spread of these sensitive regions vary substantially across tasks. GSM8K is strongly
front-loaded, with its peak in the first 8 denoising steps and a rapid decay thereafter. MMLU-Pro is
also early, but broad: its first four windows (steps 0–31) are all within 10% of its peak, after which
sensitivity declines gradually. In contrast, HumanEval is insensitive early and exhibits a broad
mid-trajectory peak, roughly around steps 40–63. Thus, a single task-independent temporal precision
schedule is unlikely to suit all tasks. Whether these sensitivity profiles can predict where additional
precision actually improves downstream performance is examined in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 A Label-Free Signal for Precision Allocation ‣ 3 Task-Dependent Precision Sensitivity Across Diffusion Steps ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="58.98" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 653.15 58.98" width="653.15"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,58.98) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#0000FF;" fill="#0000FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 8.98 L 0 50 C 0 54.96 4.02 58.98 8.98 58.98 L 644.17 58.98 C 649.13 58.98 653.15 54.96 653.15 50 L 653.15 8.98 C 653.15 4.02 649.13 0 644.17 0 L 8.98 0 C 4.02 0 0 4.02 0 8.98 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2FF;" fill="#F2F2FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.11 8.98 L 1.11 50 C 1.11 54.35 4.63 57.87 8.98 57.87 L 644.17 57.87 C 648.52 57.87 652.04 54.35 652.04 50 L 652.04 8.98 C 652.04 4.63 648.52 1.11 644.17 1.11 L 8.98 1.11 C 4.63 1.11 1.11 4.63 1.11 8.98 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 14.89 14.82)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:45.05em;--ltx-fo-height:2.54em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="37.87" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 35.18)" width="623.37"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:45.05em;">
<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00008C;">Key Takeaway<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> Sensitivity patterns differ across tasks, challenging
fixed temporal precision schedules.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.31198v1/signal_diagnostic.svg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/157;" width="476" height="157"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">KL <math id="S3.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> influence predicts where additional precision pays off.</span> Bars show the accuracy gain from upscaling a single denoising window from <math id="S3.F3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{target}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>target</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{target}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{up}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>up</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{up}}</annotation></semantics></math>, normalized by the full-precision gap (dashed line, 1.0 = full-precision accuracy), with 95% bootstrap intervals. The black curve shows the label-free proxy <math id="S3.F3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{w}I_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{w}I_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>, normalized to its peak window. <math id="S3.F3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is the Pearson correlation between the two across windows.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>A Label-Free Signal for Precision Allocation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now address RQ3: identifying, without downstream labels, which denoising windows benefit most from additional activation precision.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Denoising-window upscaling.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{target}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>target</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{target}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{up}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>up</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{up}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the target and higher activation precisions. We upscale each window <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> individually, keeping the remaining steps at <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{target}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>target</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{target}}</annotation></semantics></math> and weights fixed. The resulting gain is <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathcal{M}_{w}=\mathcal{M}_{\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{up}}(w)}-\mathcal{M}_{\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{target}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>up</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>target</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathcal{M}_{w}=\mathcal{M}_{\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{up}}(w)}-\mathcal{M}_{\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{target}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is the task metric.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">A label-free proxy.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Local divergence alone cannot capture a window’s importance: its commitments must also influence subsequent denoising <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu and Yang, 2025</a>)</cite>. Along the uniform <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{target}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>target</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{target}}</annotation></semantics></math> trajectory,
we measure full-precision divergence at committed positions <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="K_{w}=\sum_{t\in w}\sum_{j\in C_{t}}D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(p_{\mathrm{fp}}(\cdot\mid x_{t})_{j}\,\|\,p_{\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{target}}}(\cdot\mid x_{t})_{j}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></munder><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>fp</mi></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>target</mi></msub></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{w}=\sum_{t\in w}\sum_{j\in C_{t}}D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(p_{\mathrm{fp}}(\cdot\mid x_{t})_{j}\,\|\,p_{\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{target}}}(\cdot\mid x_{t})_{j}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> measures how much additional precision can change the window’s decisions. To measure how consequential those decisions are, let <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\mathrm{before}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>before</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\mathrm{before}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\mathrm{after}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>after</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\mathrm{after}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the states immediately before and after window <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> the positions still masked afterwards. The window’s downstream influence is the change in the full-precision model’s beliefs about these positions:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I_{w}=\sum_{j\in F_{w}}D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(p_{\mathrm{fp}}(\cdot\mid x_{\mathrm{after}})_{j}\,\|\,p_{\mathrm{fp}}(\cdot\mid x_{\mathrm{before}})_{j}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>fp</mi></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>after</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>fp</mi></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>before</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{w}=\sum_{j\in F_{w}}D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(p_{\mathrm{fp}}(\cdot\mid x_{\mathrm{after}})_{j}\,\|\,p_{\mathrm{fp}}(\cdot\mid x_{\mathrm{before}})_{j}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Both are computed from the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{target}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>target</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{target}}</annotation></semantics></math> trajectory alone, without labels.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For an evaluation example, let <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\mathrm{up}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>up</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\mathrm{up}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\mathrm{tg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>tg</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\mathrm{tg}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the distributions of window <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>’s commitments (token values
and positions) when the window runs at <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{up}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">A</mi><mi>up</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{up}}</annotation></semantics></math> and at <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{target}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">A</mi><mi>target</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{target}}</annotation></semantics></math> from the same state, let
<math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="J_{w}=D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\Pi_{\mathrm{up}}\|\Pi_{\mathrm{tg}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>up</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>tg</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{w}=D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\Pi_{\mathrm{up}}\|\Pi_{\mathrm{tg}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> be the range of the expected final score over the
window’s possible commitments. Then</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\Delta\mathcal{M}_{w}\right|^{2}\leq\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbb{E}[J_{w}]\,\mathbb{E}[R_{w}^{2}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\Delta\mathcal{M}_{w}\right|^{2}\leq\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbb{E}[J_{w}]\,\mathbb{E}[R_{w}^{2}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with expectations over evaluation examples.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The bound (proof in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Proof of Proposition ‣ Appendix C Theoretical Proofs ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>) separates the effect of upscaling into commitment perturbation (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>) and downstream score sensitivity (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>). Since neither is available from a single unlabeled trajectory, we use <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> as respective surrogates. Mirroring the bound, we rank windows by</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{w}=\mathbb{E}[K_{w}]\,\mathbb{E}[I_{w}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{w}=\mathbb{E}[K_{w}]\,\mathbb{E}[I_{w}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This is a motivated proxy, not a formal bound; we evaluate its predictive ability empirically.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Results.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Task-dependent sensitivity. ‣ 3.1 Temporal Sensitivity Diagnostic ‣ 3 Task-Dependent Precision Sensitivity Across Diffusion Steps ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> compares <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>
with the measured gain <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathcal{M}_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathcal{M}_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>.
On GSM8K and MMLU-Pro, the proxy correctly identifies
the most beneficial window, recovering 90% and 100%
of the full-precision gap, respectively.
The proxy closely tracks window-level gains
(<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.98" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.98</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.98</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.96" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.96</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.96</annotation></semantics></math>), including beyond the
highest-ranked window. On HumanEval, both the proxy
and observed gains concentrate in the first two windows, although measurement noise limits correlation (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.63" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.63</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.63</annotation></semantics></math>). Influence is essential: divergence alone incorrectly favors HumanEval’s final window, whose commitments cannot affect future tokens. Finally, just 16 unlabeled calibration prompts yield proxy estimates closely matching those computed from all prompts (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 0.96" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.96</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 0.96</annotation></semantics></math> across tasks; Figure <a href="#A1.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ A.1 Temporal Sensitivity Diagnostic ‣ Appendix A Further Analyses and Supplementary Results ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="75.58" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 653.15 75.58" width="653.15"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,75.58) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#0000FF;" fill="#0000FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 8.98 L 0 66.6 C 0 71.56 4.02 75.58 8.98 75.58 L 644.17 75.58 C 649.13 75.58 653.15 71.56 653.15 66.6 L 653.15 8.98 C 653.15 4.02 649.13 0 644.17 0 L 8.98 0 C 4.02 0 0 4.02 0 8.98 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2FF;" fill="#F2F2FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.11 8.98 L 1.11 66.6 C 1.11 70.95 4.63 74.48 8.98 74.48 L 644.17 74.48 C 648.52 74.48 652.04 70.95 652.04 66.6 L 652.04 8.98 C 652.04 4.63 648.52 1.11 644.17 1.11 L 8.98 1.11 C 4.63 1.11 1.11 4.63 1.11 8.98 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 14.89 14.82)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:45.05em;--ltx-fo-height:3.74em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="54.47" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 51.78)" width="623.37"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
