</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Method</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Temporal Sensitivity Diagnostic ‣ 3 Task-Dependent Precision Sensitivity Across Diffusion Steps ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> establishes that activation-quantization sensitivity varies across denoising steps and downstream tasks. Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 A Label-Free Signal for Precision Allocation ‣ 3 Task-Dependent Precision Sensitivity Across Diffusion Steps ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> further shows that a label-free signal combining quantization divergence and downstream influence can identify windows where additional precision is most beneficial. Building on these findings, we introduce <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T<sup id="S4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant</span>, which extends single-window sensitivity analysis to a task-specific activation-precision schedule under a fixed budget. Rather than designing a new quantizer, T<sup id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant uses unlabeled task-specific prompts to estimate the value of each available precision and optimizes their allocation across the denoising trajectory.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Problem Setup</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Notation and objective.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> denote a target task and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\tau}=\{s_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\tau}=\{s_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> a representative calibration set of prompts. A diffusion language model generates through <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> denoising steps, with partially generated state <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math> immediately before step <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math> be the positions committed at that step. Following the window-based analysis of Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Temporal Sensitivity Diagnostic ‣ 3 Task-Dependent Precision Sensitivity Across Diffusion Steps ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, we partition normalized denoising progress into <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> ordered, non-overlapping temporal windows <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{W}_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{W}_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, so a schedule can be applied across different step counts. Given a finite set of supported activation precisions <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> and a uniform reference precision <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{0}\in\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{0}\in\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, our task-specific schedule is <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}_{\tau}=(b_{1},\ldots,b_{K})\in\mathcal{B}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}_{\tau}=(b_{1},\ldots,b_{K})\in\mathcal{B}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, where all denoising steps in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> use <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote the additive resource cost of assigning precision <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> to window <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}(b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(b)</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., the window’s activation work multiplied by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>); the reference budget is <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{0}=\sum_{k}c_{k}(b_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{0}=\sum_{k}c_{k}(b_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. Ideally, the schedule maximizes the downstream task metric <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\tau}(\mathbf{b})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\tau}(\mathbf{b})</annotation></semantics></math> subject to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}c_{k}(b_{k})=C_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}c_{k}(b_{k})=C_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Because evaluating this objective for candidate schedules would require labeled, closed-loop generations, we instead construct a label-free surrogate.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Calibration assumption.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As in task-aware PTQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">LeVi et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we assume access to representative unlabeled calibration prompts from the target task. Following the label-free analysis of Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Task-Dependent Precision Sensitivity Across Diffusion Steps ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, T<sup id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant estimates precision importance using internal model signals, without ground-truth answers or task-specific evaluation metrics.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Quantizer-agnostic scheduling.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">T<sup id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant allocates activation precision across denoising steps without modifying the underlying quantizer or sampler. It can therefore be integrated with existing PTQ methods, including DLLMQuant <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu and Yang, 2025</a>)</cite>, FAIR-Calib <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, and STaR-Quant <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Label-Free Calibration</h3>

<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Local quantization divergence.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following the committed-position KL signal in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In A label-free proxy. ‣ 3.2 A Label-Free Signal for Precision Allocation ‣ 3 Task-Dependent Precision Sensitivity Across Diffusion Steps ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), we extend the analysis to every candidate activation precision <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}\in\mathcal{D}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}\in\mathcal{D}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>, we generate a full-precision reference trajectory and save its states <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math> and committed sets <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math>. We replay each saved state under every <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, keeping the input state fixed across precisions. Let <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\mathrm{fp}}_{i,t,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>fp</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\mathrm{fp}}_{i,t,j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{(b)}_{i,t,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{(b)}_{i,t,j}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the resulting full-precision and quantized predictive distributions at position <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. The average divergence over all positions committed in window <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{\tau,k}(b)=\frac{\displaystyle\sum_{i}\sum_{t\in\mathcal{W}_{k}}\sum_{j\in C_{i,t}}D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(p^{\mathrm{fp}}_{i,t,j}\|p^{(b)}_{i,t,j}\right)}{\displaystyle\sum_{i}\sum_{t\in\mathcal{W}_{k}}|C_{i,t}|}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>KL</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>fp</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{\tau,k}(b)=\frac{\displaystyle\sum_{i}\sum_{t\in\mathcal{W}_{k}}\sum_{j\in C_{i,t}}D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(p^{\mathrm{fp}}_{i,t,j}\|p^{(b)}_{i,t,j}\right)}{\displaystyle\sum_{i}\sum_{t\in\mathcal{W}_{k}}|C_{i,t}|}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This extends the single-precision divergence in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 A Label-Free Signal for Precision Allocation ‣ 3 Task-Dependent Precision Sensitivity Across Diffusion Steps ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> to multiple precision levels, normalized per committed position. For scheduling, the replay states come from the full-precision reference trajectory, whereas Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 A Label-Free Signal for Precision Allocation ‣ 3 Task-Dependent Precision Sensitivity Across Diffusion Steps ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> evaluates its diagnostic on the uniform-precision trajectory. Holding each replay state fixed isolates local quantization effects from changes in the generated trajectory; no task-specific token selection is required.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Downstream influence.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As demonstrated in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 A Label-Free Signal for Precision Allocation ‣ 3 Task-Dependent Precision Sensitivity Across Diffusion Steps ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, local divergence alone does not capture how a window’s commitments affect subsequent predictions. We therefore reuse the downstream-influence measure of Eq. (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In A label-free proxy. ‣ 3.2 A Label-Free Signal for Precision Allocation ‣ 3 Task-Dependent Precision Sensitivity Across Diffusion Steps ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), evaluated on the saved reference trajectory. For each prompt <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and window <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{-}_{i,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{-}_{i,k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{+}_{i,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{+}_{i,k}</annotation></semantics></math> be the states immediately before and after the window, and let <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i,k}</annotation></semantics></math> contain the positions still masked afterwards. Applying Eq. (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In A label-free proxy. ‣ 3.2 A Label-Free Signal for Precision Allocation ‣ 3 Task-Dependent Precision Sensitivity Across Diffusion Steps ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) to these states gives <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{i,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ι</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{i,k}</annotation></semantics></math>; we average across prompts as <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\iota}_{\tau,k}=N^{-1}\sum_{i}\iota_{i,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ι</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>ι</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\iota}_{\tau,k}=N^{-1}\sum_{i}\iota_{i,k}</annotation></semantics></math>. This measures the window’s downstream leverage using internal model predictions, without requiring task labels.</p>
</div>
