<p id="S4.SS3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Backtracking from <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{K}(C_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{K}(C_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> yields the selected schedule; the search also permits the all-<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> solution when the estimated gain from reallocating precision is insufficient. Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ Appendix D Algorithms ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Algorithms ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>) gives the complete budgeted solver.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2</span></span><span id="Thmproposition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Optimality of temporal allocation)<span id="Thmproposition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a finite <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> and nonnegative, integer-valued additive costs <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}(b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(b)</annotation></semantics></math>, the recurrence in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E6" title="In 4.3 Optimal Budget-Constrained Scheduling ‣ 4 Method ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) returns a globally optimal solution of Eq. (<a href="#S4.E5" title="In 4.3 Optimal Budget-Constrained Scheduling ‣ 4 Method ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) whenever the budget is feasible.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The proof follows from the principle of optimality and is given in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Proof of Proposition ‣ Appendix C Theoretical Proofs ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>. The guarantee concerns the calibrated additive surrogate, not the generally non-additive true downstream metric. Calibration produces one schedule per task; at inference, each step selects its assigned precision by a window-index lookup, with no additional diagnostic forward passes.</p>
</div>
