<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Models.</span> Three open DLMs: Dream-7B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, LLaDA-1.5-8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2026</a>)</cite> and
LLaDA-8B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
<span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmarks.</span> GSM8K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, HumanEval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, ARC-Challenge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clark et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, MMLU-Pro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, DROP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dua et al., 2019</a>)</cite> and BBH <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Suzgun et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, evaluated on held-out prompts that are disjoint from every calibration prompt.
<span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span> Full precision (FP16), round-to-nearest (RTN), and three recent post-training quantization methods for diffusion
LMs, DLLMQuant <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu and Yang, 2025</a>)</cite>, FAIR-Calib <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2026</a>)</cite> and STaR-Quant <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, each
evaluated as is and with T<sup id="S5.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant applied on top; a random schedule with the same activation
budget controls for mixed precision itself. All quantized models and every row
uses the same sampler and the same prompts, so all comparisons are paired. Splits, sampler, quantization setup, baseline implementations and
statistical tests are given in Appendix <a href="#A5.SS2" title="E.2 Main Results (Section ) ‣ Appendix E Experimental Setup and Implementation Details ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Downstream influence. ‣ 4.2 Label-Free Calibration ‣ 4 Method ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> reports W4A4 accuracy across three DLMs and six benchmarks, comparing DLLMQuant, FAIR-Calib, and STaR-Quant with and without T<sup id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant. All configurations use 4-bit weights and the same average activation precision of 4.00 bits, ensuring a fair comparison under identical bit budgets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Consistent improvements across methods and models.</span>
T<sup id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant improves average accuracy for all nine baseline/model combinations, with gains of up to 6.7 points on Dream, 3.4 on LLaDA-1.5, and 3.5 on LLaDA-8B-Instruct. Overall, 42 of 54 benchmark-level comparisons improve, with GSM8K and ARC-Challenge improving in every combination.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Best quantized accuracy across all models.</span>
T<sup id="S5.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant achieves the two highest quantized averages on every model, outperforming the strongest standalone baselines by 3.55, 1.13, and 1.40 points on Dream, LLaDA-1.5, and LLaDA-8B-Instruct, respectively. The best configurations approach FP16 accuracy, with gaps of only 2.0, 2.5, and 1.6 points.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Substantial recovery from quantization degradation.</span>
The largest gains occur on Dream, where GSM8K accuracy rises from 35.6 to 53.5 with DLLMQuant and from 46.1 to 58.8 with STaR-Quant. On GSM8K, improvements also reach 9.6 points on LLaDA-1.5 and 10.4 on LLaDA-8B-Instruct. On several benchmarks, T<sup id="S5.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant even matches or exceeds FP16 accuracy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Precision allocation matters.</span>
Random schedules with the same average bit budget perform substantially worse, falling 14–19 points below the original baselines on Dream. In contrast, T<sup id="S5.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant achieves the best quantized average on every model, highlighting the importance of allocating precision to the appropriate denoising steps.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Ablation Study</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.3 Optimal Budget-Constrained Scheduling ‣ 4 Method ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> evaluates three aspects of T<sup id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant on LLaDA-8B-Instruct and GSM8K under W4A4 (uniform W4A4: 44.1, T<sup id="S5.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant: 49.4, FP16: 52.9). <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a) Calibration size:</span> Just 16 prompts recover roughly 75% of the full gain, with stable schedules consistently prioritizing early denoising steps. <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(b) Tokens per step:</span> The schedule transfers from 4 to 2 tokens per step, maintaining an approximately 5-point improvement, while gains vanish at 8 tokens per step as overall accuracy deteriorates. <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(c) Activation budget:</span> T<sup id="S5.SS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant achieves 48.4% accuracy with only 3.75 average bits, outperforming uniform W4A4 (44.1%) despite using fewer bits. Moreover, T<sup id="S5.SS3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant consistently outperforms random schedules at the same bit budget, while increasing precision beyond 4 bits provides negligible additional benefit. These results demonstrate that effective precision allocation matters more than simply increasing the bit budget. Further ablations, including window size, precision levels, the set of activation widths (Table <a href="#A2.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Precision set (Table ). ‣ Appendix B Additional Ablation Studies ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) additional quantization budgets, and calibration signals, are provided in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Additional Ablation Studies ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our findings demonstrate that quantization sensitivity in DLMs depends on both the downstream task and the denoising step, challenging uniform precision allocation. Building on this observation, we introduced T<sup id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant, a task- and time-aware mixed-precision framework that predicts precision importance using unlabeled calibration prompts and allocates activation precision under a fixed bit-width budget. Across three DLMs and six benchmarks, T<sup id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant consistently improves average accuracy when integrated with existing quantization methods, with gains of up to 6.7 percentage points under W4A4. These results highlight the importance of considering both task requirements and temporal generation dynamics when quantizing DLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By improving accuracy under fixed precision budgets, T<sup id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant offers a promising direction for making DLM deployment more computationally efficient and accessible, potentially reducing memory and energy requirements. Its compatibility with existing quantization methods may further facilitate practical adoption. However, quantization can also alter model behavior beyond benchmark accuracy, including safety-related responses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hong et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Egashira et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Future work should therefore examine the effects of task- and time-aware precision allocation on safety, robustness, and real-world inference efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Limitations</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although T<sup id="Sx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant demonstrates strong results across three DLMs, six benchmarks, and three quantization methods, further evaluation of deployment-related aspects, including hardware efficiency (inference speed and energy consumption) and potential impacts on model safety, remains for future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">LLM Usage Statement</h2>

<div id="Sx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We used LLMs during manuscript preparation mainly as writing assistants, for grammar checking and for improving the clarity and naturalness of the text. All LLM-generated suggestions were manually reviewed and edited by the authors. LLMs did not play any important role in developing the core methods, experiments, analysis, or conclusions of this research.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Ethical Considerations</h2>

<div id="Sx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work aims to improve the efficiency and accessibility of diffusion language models through quantization. While quantization may affect model safety and robustness, investigating these effects is left for future work.</p>
</div>
