<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Further Analyses and Supplementary Results</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Temporal Sensitivity Diagnostic</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We repeat the label-free diagnostic of Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Task-Dependent Precision Sensitivity Across Diffusion Steps ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> on two further masked-diffusion LMs: LLaDA-1.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2026</a>)</cite> and Dream-v0-Instruct-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generation:</span> 512 tokens, 128 denoising steps in a single block, greedy decoding.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quantization:</span> the same W4A4 recipe (rotation, GPTQ W4, per-token A4 with clip 0.7) and calibration data.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tasks:</span> the same subsets, prompts and scorers.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Each model is quantized with a single rotation seed.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.31198v1/calib_size_ablation.svg" id="A1.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/533;" width="476" height="533"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span><span id="A1.F5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A few unlabeled prompts suffice.</span> Proxy computed from <math id="A1.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> random calibration prompts. <em id="A1.F5.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Top:</em> correlation with the all-prompt proxy. <em id="A1.F5.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Bottom:</em> correlation with held-out accuracy gains.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LLaDA-1.5.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLaDA-1.5 is LLaDA-8B-Instruct further trained with variance-reduced preference optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Its architecture, tokenizer, sampler and EOS rules are unchanged. Figure <a href="#A1.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ LLaDA-1.5. ‣ A.1 Temporal Sensitivity Diagnostic ‣ Appendix A Further Analyses and Supplementary Results ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows the resulting profiles, which closely match those of LLaDA-8B-Instruct (Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
The Pearson correlation between the two models’ per-window profiles is 0.99 (GSM8K), 0.98 (HumanEval) and 0.92 (MMLU-Pro).</p>
<ul id="A1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GSM8K:</span> damage peaks in the first window and decays.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HumanEval:</span> damage rises to a broad peak over steps 40–64.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MMLU-Pro:</span> damage forms a broad early plateau, peaks at steps 16–23 and rises again in the last window.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">LLaDA-1.5 is a fine-tuned LLaDA-8B, so this result shows that the temporal profile survives preference alignment.
It does not test other architectures; Dream addresses that below.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.31198v1/kl_diagnostic_llada15.svg" id="A1.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/339;" width="476" height="339"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Label-free diagnostic for LLaDA-1.5 under W4A4, computed as in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>: score-relevant KL per 8-step window, normalized to each task’s peak, with 95% problem-bootstrap bands.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dream-v0-Instruct-7B.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Dream uses its own sampler <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which commits the lowest-entropy masked positions first.
Everything else follows the shared setup; only the handling of the end-of-sequence (EOS) token differs.
At the first denoising steps Dream predicts EOS at almost every masked position, so the EOS rule matters more than for LLaDA:</p>
<ul id="A1.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">No EOS rule (Dream’s default):</span> padding is committed first, and the answer is squeezed into the last few steps.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLaDA’s rules:</span> the few non-EOS positions are forced in first regardless of their confidence.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Our choice:</span> the confidence of positions predicting EOS or end-of-turn is lowered by <math id="A1.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math> nat, on all tasks.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We chose <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, and entropy over top-2-margin ordering, among six variants by full-precision accuracy on the first 20 problems of each task.
The criterion was fixed before any damage profile was computed.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The profiles in Figure <a href="#A1.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Dream-v0-Instruct-7B. ‣ A.1 Temporal Sensitivity Diagnostic ‣ Appendix A Further Analyses and Supplementary Results ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> remain task-dependent, but the tasks peak at different points in denoising than on LLaDA.</p>
<ul id="A1.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GSM8K:</span> Dream answers directly under the shared answer-only few-shot prompt (about six tokens), so the damage concentrates in the first window, with a small rise in the last one.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HumanEval:</span> damage stays low until step 64, then rises to a peak at steps 96–104, late rather than mid-trajectory as on LLaDA.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I4.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MMLU-Pro:</span> after a first-window spike (most W4A4 responses state the answer first), damage ramps up steadily to the end.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.31198v1/kl_diagnostic_dream.svg" id="A1.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/339;" width="476" height="339"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Label-free diagnostic for Dream-v0-Instruct-7B under W4A4, computed as in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>: score-relevant KL per 8-step window, normalized to each task’s peak, with 95% problem-bootstrap bands.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
