<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Additional Ablation Studies</h2>

<figure id="A2.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.31198v1/ablations_more_gsm8k.svg" id="A2.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/273;" width="476" height="273"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Further ablations on LLaDA-8B-Instruct, GSM8K (<math id="A2.F8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=512</annotation></semantics></math>; bars are 95% intervals): (a) steps per window, (b) number of 8-bit windows
<math id="A2.F8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, the circle marking the allocation’s own choice. Orange: uniform W4A4; dashed: FP16.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Window size (Figure <a href="#A2.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Appendix B Additional Ablation Studies ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>a).</h4>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From 16 steps per window down to a single step the accuracy stays within 1.7 points (47.7 to 49.4), and the coarsest windows are as good as the
finest: the schedule needs few decisions, not many.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Number of 8-bit windows (Figure <a href="#A2.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Appendix B Additional Ablation Studies ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>b).</h4>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With three levels and a fixed 4.00-bit average, one 8-bit window is paid for by four 3-bit windows, so every schedule is described by the number
<math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> of 8-bit windows. Forcing <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=3</annotation></semantics></math> instead of the allocation’s own choice <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=2</annotation></semantics></math> costs 1.0 and 3.5 points, so the allocation lands on
the optimum of this one-parameter family. For every <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> its schedule coincides with a calibration-free prior (8 bits on the first <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> windows,
3 bits on the last <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4m</annotation></semantics></math>); on GSM8K, choosing <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is where the calibration earns its keep.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Precision set (Table <a href="#A2.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Precision set (Table ). ‣ Appendix B Additional Ablation Studies ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</h4>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Allowing all six widths <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{3,4,5,6,7,8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{3,4,5,6,7,8\}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of the four of the main table, <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{3,4,6,8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{3,4,6,8\}</annotation></semantics></math>, changes the accuracy little: over the 36
composed cells of Table <a href="#A2.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Precision set (Table ). ‣ Appendix B Additional Ablation Studies ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the mean rises from 45.7 to 46.3, by 0.6 points on GSM8K, 0.1 on ARC-C and MMLU-Pro,
and 1.4 on HumanEval, whose 100-problem cells account for the largest single changes. A T<sup id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant row is the best quantized result in 11 of the 12 model–benchmark cells. The method therefore does not depend on a particular choice of intermediate widths; we keep <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{3,4,6,8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{3,4,6,8\}</annotation></semantics></math> in the
main experiments because each additional width costs one more replay of the calibration trajectories.</p>
</div>
