<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Other precisions.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A3.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ C.2 Proof of Proposition ‣ Appendix C Theoretical Proofs ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> applies T<sup id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant at two other budgets on LLaDA-1.5-8B and Dream-7B-Instruct. At W8A8 (8-bit weights, 8.00 average
activation bits over the widths <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{4,8,16\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{4,8,16\}</annotation></semantics></math>) uniform quantization is already at full precision on every cell, and the schedule is a harmless
no-op: one gain (LLaDA-1.5 GSM8K, +3.5, <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.04" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.04</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.04</annotation></semantics></math>), otherwise ties, pooled 132 / 123. At W4A3 (3.00 average bits) uniform quantization
collapses and the random schedule is catastrophic; the schedule recovers most of the loss, by up to 32 points on Dream ARC-C and 8.6 points on
LLaDA-1.5 GSM8K, pooled 371 / 133 with the widths <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{2,3,4\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{2,3,4\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{2,3,8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{2,3,8\}</annotation></semantics></math> set is worse at this budget because one 8-bit window costs five
2-bit windows, and on two LLaDA-1.5 cells the allocation then declines to trade at all.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Proxy signal and aggregation (Table <a href="#A2.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Proxy signal and aggregation (Table ). ‣ Appendix B Additional Ablation Studies ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</h4>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On the window-upscaling experiments of Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 A Label-Free Signal for Precision Allocation ‣ 3 Task-Dependent Precision Sensitivity Across Diffusion Steps ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, we compare the proxy <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{w}=\mathbb{E}[K_{w}]\,\mathbb{E}[I_{w}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{w}=\mathbb{E}[K_{w}]\,\mathbb{E}[I_{w}]</annotation></semantics></math> with
three alternatives. The per-example form <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\sqrt{K_{w}I_{w}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\sqrt{K_{w}I_{w}}]</annotation></semantics></math> suggested by the proof ranks windows no better on any task and worse on
MMLU-Pro (<math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.85" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.85</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.85</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.96" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.96</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.96</annotation></semantics></math>). Divergence alone fails on HumanEval (<math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=-0.08" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.08</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=-0.08</annotation></semantics></math>). Influence alone ranks windows nearly as well as <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>, so most
of the ranking comes from <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>; the divergence term is what the allocation needs to price each precision level, which influence cannot do.</p>
</div>
