<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Experimental Setup and Implementation Details</h2>

<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Model, Sampler, and Quantization</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unless stated otherwise, all experiments use LLaDA-8B-Instruct with 512-token generations over 128 denoising steps in a single
512-token block, so four positions are committed at each step. We use the standard low-confidence remasking sampler <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>)</cite> with
temperature 0 and no classifier-free guidance, so decoding is deterministic. End-of-sequence handling follows the official
LLaDA evaluation settings for each benchmark.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Rotations follow QuaRot <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ashkboos et al., 2024</a>)</cite>: the residual stream is rotated by a random Hadamard matrix, and online Hadamard transforms are applied
before the attention output and MLP down projections. Weights of the transformer linear layers are quantized to 4 bits with
GPTQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Frantar et al., 2023</a>)</cite> (per output channel, asymmetric, MSE-optimal clipping, act-order), calibrated on 128 WikiText <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merity et al., 2016</a>)</cite> sequences of 512 tokens with
random masking. The embeddings and language-model head remain in high precision. Activations are quantized dynamically per
token, with a clipping ratio of 0.7, at the inputs to the QKV projection, the attention output projection, the MLP up/gate
projections, and the MLP down projection. Attention scores and softmax remain unquantized. We refer to the configuration with
4-bit weights and 4-bit activations as W4A4. The reference model uses BF16 computation, denoted fp16 throughout for
consistency. The random Hadamard matrix is drawn from a seed, which we refer to as the quantization seed: different seeds give different W4A4
models with the same configuration. The window-upscaling experiments of Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 A Label-Free Signal for Precision Allocation ‣ 3 Task-Dependent Precision Sensitivity Across Diffusion Steps ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> average the measured gains
over five quantization seeds for GSM8K, ten for HumanEval and one for MMLU-Pro; all other experiments use a single seed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span>Main Results (Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Main Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>)</h3>

<section id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Models.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Dream-7B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2025</a>)</cite> (<span id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Dream-org/Dream-v0-Instruct-7B</span>), LLaDA-1.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>
(<span id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GSAI-ML/LLaDA-1.5</span>) and LLaDA-8B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>)</cite> (<span id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GSAI-ML/LLaDA-8B-Instruct</span>), with their released
weights and chat templates. All three are masked-diffusion LMs that decode a
fixed-length block by iteratively unmasking tokens; they differ in pre-training data, alignment recipe (LLaDA-1.5 adds preference
optimization) and, for Dream, in architecture and initialization, which makes Dream the most sensitive of the three to activation
quantization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Benchmarks and splits.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Benchmarks and splits. ‣ E.2 Main Results (Section ) ‣ Appendix E Experimental Setup and Implementation Details ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> lists the benchmarks, the number of held-out problems, the number of calibration prompts, and the output
length. For each benchmark the problems are drawn once from the original evaluation set and split into a calibration pool and a held-out
evaluation pool; the calibration pool is the only data any calibrated method (the baselines and T<sup id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant) sees, and the held-out pool is the
same for every row, so all comparisons in the paper are paired by problem. Accuracy is exact match of the extracted final answer after normalization (GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, MMLU-Pro, BBH; for DROP, exact match under the official DROP answer normalization against any annotated answer span, on the validation split, rather than the usual F1) or
pass@1 of the program on the benchmark’s tests (HumanEval).
Averages are unweighted means over benchmarks.</p>
</div>
