<section id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Sampler.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Every row uses the models’ upstream sampler unchanged: 4 tokens are unmasked per step with low-confidence remasking, at temperature 0, with
the output lengths of Table <a href="#A5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Benchmarks and splits. ‣ E.2 Main Results (Section ) ‣ Appendix E Experimental Setup and Implementation Details ‣ Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Rows therefore differ only in the quantization applied to the network.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Quantization setup.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Quantization is simulated (the quantized integer values are used in bf16 arithmetic), the standard protocol for post-training quantization
studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ashkboos et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Frantar et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Weights are quantized to 4 bits per channel with GPTQ after a Hadamard rotation of the hidden dimension (QuaRot-style, including
the online rotation of the attention-output input). Activations, the inputs of all linear layers, are quantized dynamically per token; for
widths below 8 bits the per-token range is clipped to 70% of its maximum. This rotated, GPTQ-quantized W4 model with per-token A4
activations is the shared base of every quantized row except RTN, which uses round-to-nearest 4-bit per-channel weights and per-token A4
activations without rotation or GPTQ. T<sup id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant keeps the base and varies only the activation width along the denoising trajectory: the
trajectory is divided into 16 windows, each window uses one of {3, 4, 6, 8} bits, and the allocation is chosen on the calibration pool under
an exact average of 4.00 bits (the method section gives the allocation). The random schedule assigns window widths at random under the same
4.00-bit average; one fixed draw (seed 0) is used for every model and benchmark.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">DLLMQuant <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu and Yang, 2025</a>)</cite>, FAIR-Calib <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2026</a>)</cite> and STaR-Quant <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2026</a>)</cite> are
reimplemented on the common rotated W4 base, so that every row shares the same weights, sampler and
prompts and only the activation treatment differs: DLLMQuant’s calibration over sampled denoising states, FAIR-Calib’s calibration prior, and
STaR-Quant’s training-free state-aware channel scaling with its attention correction are each calibrated on denoising states produced by the
full-precision model from the calibration prompts. “Base + T<sup id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Quant” applies our window allocation on top of the baseline’s calibration,
which is left unchanged.</p>
</div>
</section>
